{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788384630,"nonce":"hjKdltkMu1bOCN7hI3IjqARfq9I=","title":"`baseviz view` prints the grid **vertically mirrored** while its own header","message":"says \"north up\". Row 0 of the IR is NORTH (pinned, `bac4eba`,\n`templates/INDEX.md`), and the view puts row 0 at the BOTTOM.\n\n\nA deliberately asymmetric 3x4 IR: `Bed` on grid row 1 (second from north),\n`Door` on grid row 3 (south wall).\n\n    layers[0] = [[\"Wall\",\"Wall\",\"Wall\"],       \u003c- row 0, NORTH\n                 [\"Bed\",\".\",\"Wall\"],\n                 [\".\",\".\",\"Wall\"],\n                 [\"Wall\",\"Door\",\"Wall\"]]       \u003c- row 3, SOUTH\n\n    $ python3 -m baseviz view probe.ir.json\n    AR_OrientProbe  3x4  (composite; north up; top-of-stack, conduit underlaid)\n        0 1 2\n      3|##++##      \u003c- the DOOR, i.e. grid row 3 (SOUTH), printed FIRST/TOP\n      2|    ##\n      1|BD  ##\n      0|######      \u003c- grid row 0 (NORTH) printed LAST/BOTTOM\n\n\n`baseviz/canvas.py:245`\n\n    for r in range(H - 1, -1, -1):\n        out.append(f\"{r:3d}|\" + \"\".join(cell(x, r) for x in range(W)))\n\nIt walks rows from `H-1` down to `0`, so the highest row index prints at the\ntop. That is the correct convention for a Z-COORDINATE axis (z increases\nnorthward, so high z belongs at the top) — but these are GRID ROW indices, and\nthe pin says grid row 0 is north. The loop is treating `r` as a z offset.\n\n\nThe header asserts \"north up\", so a reader has no cue that the picture is\nflipped. Everything about a base layout that has an orientation reads wrong:\nwhich wall the door is on, which side a bench's interaction cell faces, whether\na `Cooler` vents indoors or outdoors, which room the sun hits. The corpus is\nfull of orientation-load-bearing detail for exactly this reason —\n`templates/INDEX.md` records that pinning row 0 as north is what caught\n`freezer-kitchen`'s two `Cooler_North` cells and the `FueledStove_South`\ninteraction-cell bug. A viewer that mirrors the grid undoes that check.\n\nCaught in run openrun-20260902 while designing a replacement base: the whole\n27x27 layout was reviewed through this view and read as correct.\n\n\nEither iterate `range(H)` so row 0 prints first, or keep the loop and relabel\nthe axis so the number shown is a z offset rather than a row index. The first\nis almost certainly right — `render.py` already documents \"row 0 is z = oz + h\n- 1\" and `map-dump` publishes `north_up: true`, so `view` is the only surface\nin the repo that disagrees with the pin.\n\n\n- The probe IR above renders with the `Door` row at the BOTTOM and the `Bed`\n  row second from the TOP.\n- The row labels and the header agree with `baseviz/ir.py`'s pinned docstring;\n  whichever convention is chosen, the header states it explicitly rather than\n  saying \"north up\" over an unlabelled flip.\n- A regression check lands with the fix — an asymmetric fixture is enough, and\n  it is the only thing that would have caught this.\n- `templates/*.ir.json` are re-read through the corrected view once, since any\n  template reviewed only through `view` was reviewed mirrored.","files":null}]}